 "title": "High bar: jump to the bar and giant swing",
 "category": "parkour-apparatus-balance",
 "time_limit_s": 60.0,
 "stages": [
  "jumped",
  "caught the bar",
  "over the top",
  "full circle"
 ],
 "rulebook": "FIG Apparatus Norms, men's artistic gymnastics, horizontal bar: the bar 2.80 m above the floor (2.60 m above the 0.20 m landing mats), 2.40 m between the uprights, 28 mm in diameter, fixed in the uprights; FIG Code of Points (men's artistic gymnastics), horizontal bar: the giant swing (a full circle round the bar with the body extended, in hang)",
 "deviations": [
  "the grip (the hands on the bar): each hand is a two-finger gripper, Unitree's Dex1-1 (90 mm stroke, 80 mm jaws with rubber pads; sold for the G1), closed across the bar; it holds the bar by the jaws' friction alone, nothing is welded, and the bar can slide along the jaws and out of them; the bar is fixed, as the norm's is, and a hand turns on it only by its pads sliding round it or by the wrist's joints",
  "the grip force: each jaw clamps with up to 528.0 N, 4.4 times the Dex1-1's 120 N, because the device's force holds only 1.41 body weights with both hands (measured) while the bottom of a giant swing pulls 4.2 body weights or more (4.75 to 5.8 measured in this venue; gymnasts 3.5 to 3.7, Kopp & Reid 1980): 533 N per jaw is that load's need with a margin of 1.5. The device's own rating is a 5 kg friction-grasp load: this hand is far stronger than any Unitree hand",
  "the grip (the hands stay on the bar): defined by contact: a hand holds the bar while the bar touches both of its gripper's jaws; after the catch a break of up to 0.05 s (the pads' contact flickering under a load) is not letting go",
  "the mount: the robot (1.32 m) starts standing on a 1.0 m mounting block under the bar and jumps to it (a rise of about 0.15 m), because it cannot reach a 2.80 m bar from the mats (a gymnast is lifted to the bar or jumps from a springboard)",
  "FIG Code of Points (the body extended, judged by eye): the extension is measured from the wrists: whenever the centre of mass is above the bar it must be at least 0.565 m from the midpoint of the wrists (nine tenths of the dead hang's 0.628 m), because the referee has positions, not a judge's eye, and the grippers may fold at the wrists on the fixed bar",
  "FIG Apparatus Norms, horizontal bar (elasticity): the bar is rigid, not the norm's elastic steel bar on tensioned cables, because the simulator has rigid bodies only (the real bar's spring helps the gymnast: this is not easier)",
  "FIG Code of Points (a routine is scored): one element is judged pass or fail, because the task is a single action"
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Standing on the block under the high bar, jump up and catch the bar with both grippers, then do one giant swing: take your extended body in a full circle around the bar, without letting go.",
  "rules": "The bar is a competition horizontal bar: 0.028 m thick, 2.8 m above the floor, 2.4 m long between its two uprights, along the y axis; it is fixed and does not turn. Your hands are two-finger grippers (each jaw clamps with up to 528.0 N); they hold the bar by the friction of their jaws only, so the bar can slide along the jaws and out of them. A hand holds the bar while the bar touches both of that gripper's jaws. You start standing on the block and must jump to the bar: the catch is the moment both hands hold the bar after you have left the block (holding it before leaving the block fails). Before the catch you fail if you touch the mats or the floor; you may land back on the block and jump again. After the catch, when for more than 0.05 s a hand has not held the bar you have let go (whether you opened the gripper or the bar slipped out), and there is no regrip; only the grippers may touch the bar; touching the block, the uprights, the mats or the floor fails. Your swing angle is the angle between straight down and the line from the bar to your centre of mass, seen from the side. The giant swing is a full circle of that line after the catch: the angle must travel 360.0 degrees in one direction (forwards or backwards) without turning back; a turn back of more than 5.0 degrees starts the count again from where you turned. The body must be extended over the top: whenever your centre of mass is higher than the bar it must be at least 0.565 m from the midpoint of your two wrists (nine tenths of the dead hang's 0.628 m: a slight pike or arch passes, a tuck or bent arms do not); if it comes closer there, the count starts again from that point. You succeed the moment the count reaches 360.0 degrees. Below the bar you may bend as you like to build up the swing. You fail after 60 s.",
  "venue": "At t = 0 you stand on a 1.0 m block (0.40 m along x, 0.80 m along y) under the middle of the bar, facing +x: both arms straight up, the wrists turned so that each gripper's jaws open fore and aft, the fingers pointing up, the grippers fully open (grip values 0), the bar about 0.15 m above the grippers' bodies and right above the gaps between the jaws (the jaws' centres at y = +0.207 and -0.207 m). A grip value is that gripper's closure command (0 open, 1 closed): on the 28 mm bar, about 0.82 or more clamps it at the full force, below about 0.69 the jaws no longer clamp it; a full close takes 0.24 s. The bar is not a free body: the `held` flags of your state stay empty for it; the grip is judged as the rules say. The `stand` action keeps the legs in the stance; `joints` or `lowcmd` rows drive every joint. The uprights stand at the two ends of the bar, 1.2 m to either side of you; the landing mats (0.20 m) cover the floor under the bar around the block.",
  "difficulty": "A real jump: the legs must lift the whole robot about 0.15 m so that the bar enters the open jaws, and the jaws must close in flight. Then nothing pushes you: the swing has to be pumped up from a hang by the legs, the waist and the shoulders, against motors of 88 N m at the hips and 25 N m in the arms, until the body goes over the top of the bar extended; the bottom of a giant swing pulls more than four times your weight through the grippers and the arms, and the grippers slide round the fixed bar against their own friction."
 },
